65094 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065095 #edb/ref 1061 16963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065095 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "027aea00-6bd5-5235-9135-7e82295e6484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065095 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065281 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065281 #edb/ref 1061 77819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065281 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39aa10e9-422a-5c1c-b5b2-de119dcca067" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065281 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065282 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065282 #edb/ref 1061 77820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065282 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46d3cb51-86b4-59d6-8a39-eab709bb05a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065282 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065283 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065283 #edb/ref 1061 77824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065283 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2792086-a352-5ca3-b086-f381a58864aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065283 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065284 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065284 #edb/ref 1061 77825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065284 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "503ce197-02ae-5ae2-897a-80af7de15a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065284 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065285 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065285 #edb/ref 1061 77828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065285 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b129822d-f637-5611-9cad-e2f8b6aefca4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065285 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065286 #edb/ref 1061 77829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065286 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ddc832d-59f9-5fa2-85e2-002f7b9c4ccc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065286 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1064 "Multiplying any number by zero *always* equals zero. Below are some examples.\n\n- $5 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $123\\times 0 = 0$\n- $-314\\,159 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $0\\times 0 = 0$\n\nTo see *why* multiplying any number by zero always equals zero, let's take a look at the multiplication table for $5$, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\cdot 4 &= 20 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 3 &= 15 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 2 &= 10 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 1 &= 5 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0 &= ◻\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is reduced by $5$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\square = 0\n$$\n\n.\n\nNote that, since multiplication is commutative, multiplying zero by any number (including itself) equals zero as well. Some examples are shown below.\n\n- $0 \\times 5 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 123 = 0$\n- $0 \\times -314\\,159 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 0 = 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1065 3119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7ed778da-8d5a-5363-972b-d9435e22a0e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1064 "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\div 2 = 0$\n- $0\\div (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1065 3120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "65c0e3ec-7962-5911-a3c9-9de126e87edc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing Zero by Another Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1064 "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n6\\div 3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n6 = 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nHowever, this argument does not work if we divide by zero. Suppose we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nand this relationship cannot be true because any number multiplied by zero is zero! Therefore, $$? is not a number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1065 3121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d8152649-f215-53b2-9811-ce74f0d61dc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing by Zero is Undefined" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065416 #edb/ref 1064 "Dividing with negative numbers follows similar rules as multiplying with negative numbers. \n\nFirst of all, a negative number divided by a positive number gives a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(+)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's use this rule to find the value of $(-6) \\div 2.$\n\nHere, since we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-6)$ by a positive number ${\\color{blue}(2)},$ the result is going to be negative:\n![](images/b6/b64108fa03fc286ddcd04ac4cd7a9d77c1672d0ced66ebb1953c97afa5488533.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $6$ by $2$ *without* any signs: $6 \\div 2 = 3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\div {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} -3 }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065416 #edb/ref 1065 8108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065416 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b92ac2e4-b983-5fd5-bc18-7dfb821497f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065416 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065417 #edb/ref 1064 "A positive number divided by a negative number is also a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(+)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, to find $8\\div (-2),$ we have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(8)$ by a negative one ${\\color{red}(-2)}.$ So the result will be negative:\n![](images/79/797eda7126fff569fccda2681c628f99f103057d7c5e23c1b65cb566b48ef203.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $8$ by $2$ without any signs: $8 \\div 2 = 4.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\div {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}-4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065417 #edb/ref 1065 8109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065417 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "67beb927-7da4-5576-8982-1a823c878691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065417 #edb/ref 1067 "The Quotient of a Positive and a Negative is a Negative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065418 #edb/ref 1064 "Finally, the third rule is that a negative number divided by a negative number is a positive number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $-12 \\div (-3).$ We have to divide a negative number $\\color{red}(-12)$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-3)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n![](images/d3/d31776de6ab95f8178d0bdd6e3b34cfa2f4fc0b987851dcace50aff1818936da.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $12$ by $3$ without any signs: $12 \\div 3 = 4.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue}4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065418 #edb/ref 1065 8111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065418 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b1d3d95e-7953-5c5b-b97b-d4eb85e1f80d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065418 #edb/ref 1067 "The Quotient of a Negative and a Negative is a Positive" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072476 #edb/ref